## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Add trustworthy time-bounded operational trends for App Server behavior and Tool Shed work
efficiency, together with privacy-bounded exports of exactly the displayed data. Replace isolated
latest totals with interpretable changes over time while preserving measurement coverage,
counter-epoch boundaries, platform/version cohorts, and explicit unknowns.

### Why It Matters

Current App Server counters are updated in place, and Work Efficiency is presented as a list of
metric changes. These surfaces can reveal a current value but make it difficult to answer whether
failure rate, fallback use, remedial effort, duration, or retry behavior is improving or regressing.
Naive charts or project rankings would be worse than no analytics when token coverage or counter
epochs differ.

### Desired Outcome

The operator can select a bounded time range and inspect App Server attempts, failures, fallback
rate, recurring failure groups, remedial tokens where measured, coverage, interactions, output
bytes, duration, and retries by project/instance/version/platform. Every chart exposes freshness,
coverage, sample window, and epoch/reset boundaries. Exports contain the same allowlisted rows and
active filters visible on screen, with schema version and generation timestamp.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Never rank or compare projects as if partial token coverage were complete.
- Preserve counter epochs and reset boundaries; do not join incompatible windows into a smooth
  but false trend.
- Store change-only or bounded periodic observations, not one permanent history row per heartbeat.
- Use explicit retention and aggregation limits. PostgreSQL growth must remain measurable and
  recoverable.
- Exports may contain only the allowlisted hosted projection—never prompts, source, paths,
  commands, raw failures, credentials, or local database rows.
- Do not introduce a general business-intelligence platform, arbitrary query builder, or public
  analytics API.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Change-only metric snapshots | Matches semantic reporting and limits duplicate history | Irregular sampling needs clear chart semantics | Preferred foundation |
| Fixed periodic rollups | Predictable chart intervals | Creates rows without semantic change and needs aggregation policy | Consider for server-side derived rollups |
| Raw event/heartbeat retention | Maximum reconstruction | Excessive volume and privacy/cost risk | Reject |
| CSV/JSON export of current filtered view | Portable and auditable | Needs schema/version and row caps | Preferred after view semantics settle |

## Open Questions

- Which default windows are useful: 24 hours, 7 days, 30 days, or release-to-release?
- Should rates be derived from cumulative counter deltas server-side, or reported as bounded local
  window aggregates?
- What retention and downsampling policy preserves meaningful trends without permanent high-volume
  telemetry?
- Which comparisons should be blocked or visibly qualified when coverage, platform, version, or
  epoch differs?
- Are CSV and JSON both needed initially, and what row/size cap keeps export safe?
- How should release markers appear on charts so regressions can be correlated without claiming
  causation?

## Decisions

- Treat coverage, freshness, window, and counter epoch as first-class data, not footnotes.
- Prefer change-only observations and server-derived bounded views; reject heartbeat history.
- Exports must reproduce the visible filtered projection with a versioned schema and explicit cap.
- Avoid league tables, scores, or unsupported causal claims.

## Don't Forget

- App Server failures mean failure of the App Server path, not a campaign or product test failure
  after healthy execution.
- Work Efficiency proxy fields remain separate from measured provider tokens.
- A flat line may mean no semantic change, no report, quiescence, or missing coverage; the UI must
  distinguish those conditions.
- Release markers are useful context but do not prove that a release caused a metric change.

